.TH TAIL 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
tail \- output the last part of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
.RB [ \-n
.RI [ + ] lines ]
.RB [ \-c
.RI [ + ] bytes ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tail
writes the last 10 lines of each
.I file
to standard output. With no file, or for a file named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input. With more than one file, each is headed by
.BI "==> " name " <=="
(standard input is named
.BR "standard input" ),
with a blank line before every heading but the first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " lines"
The last
.I lines
lines.
.RI + lines
starts at line
.I lines
instead (1 is the first), and goes on to the end.
.TP
.BI \-c " bytes"
The last
.I bytes
bytes;
.RI + bytes
starts at that byte and goes on to the end.
.TP
.BI \- number
The same as
.B \-n
.IR number .
.PP
The number may be joined to its option
.RB ( \-n5 )
or follow it as the next argument. A later
.B \-n
undoes an earlier
.BR \-c .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a number is invalid or a file can't be opened (the
other files are still shown).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tail \-n 3 /init.lua
tail \-n +2 list.txt
ps | tail \-c 100
.fi
.SH NOTES
There is no
.B \-f
(follow). An argument that isn't one of these options is taken as a file
name.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR grep (1)
